(A declaration that lands on an already-used slot names a fresh buffer, so
 redeclaring `scratch` every iteration after freeing it the round before
 gets a brand-new, working buffer each time - not the freed one's empty
 header. Already true before this fix; pinned here alongside the rest of
 the after-Free matrix.)

a number called n is 0.
While n is less than 3, a buffer called scratch is "round {n}", Print scratch as text, Free scratch, increment n.
Print "done".
